---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeKitchenConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeKitchenConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="grams-to-cups">
  <UnitsLifeKitchenConverter client:load mode="grams-to-cups" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>There is no universal grams-per-cup</h2>
    <p>
      Search for "how many grams in a cup" and you get 128, 240 or another single number. Each is wrong
      for almost all ingredients, because a cup measures volume and a gram measures mass. To convert grams
      to cups you need the density, and density is a property of the ingredient.
    </p>
    <p>One US customary cup (236.6 mL) holds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>237 g of water</li>
      <li>197 g of granulated sugar</li>
      <li>123 g of spooned all-purpose flour</li>
      <li>111 g of grated cheddar</li>
      <li>84 g of cocoa powder</li>
      <li>30 g of raw spinach leaves</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The heaviest is nearly eight times the lightest, so this converter asks for the ingredient first.
    </p>

    <h2>The flour problem</h2>
    <p>
      This matters most for flour, and the variation comes from how you fill the cup. Flour spooned
      lightly into the cup and levelled off is about 125 g. A cup dipped into the bag compacts it to
      nearly 145 g. That is a <strong>16% difference</strong> in the main structural ingredient of a
      bake, and the most common reason a recipe that works for its author gives dry cookies for others.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ingredient picker lists both versions. If a recipe does not say which method it used, assume
      spooned. That is the convention in American food publishing.
    </p>

    <h2>Which cup are you holding?</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Cup</th><th scope="col">Volume</th><th scope="col">Where</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>US customary</td><td>236.588 mL</td><td>American recipes, measuring cup sets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>US legal</td><td>240 mL</td><td>Nutrition labelling, some modern cookbooks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Metric</td><td>250 mL</td><td>Australia, New Zealand, some of Canada</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Imperial</td><td>284.131 mL</td><td>Pre-metric British books</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Japanese (gou-based)</td><td>200 mL</td><td>Japanese rice cookers and recipes</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Smallest to largest is a 20% spread. The selector at the top of the tool changes all numbers on
      the page, including the reference tables.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the density numbers come from</h2>
    <p>
      Each ingredient is stored as grams per 240 mL, the reference most published US charts use. Other
      cup sizes, tablespoons, teaspoons and millilitres come from that by volume. So the page stays
      consistent: switch to Australian cups and the tablespoon figures change with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are typical values for a normally handled ingredient. Grind, humidity, brand and how firmly
      you pack the cup all change them, so each ingredient with a large handling difference is listed
      twice.
    </p>

    <h2>Our recommendation</h2>
    <p>
      Buy a digital scale. A volume measurement of a solid estimates its weight from an assumed packing
      density. A scale removes the assumption, uses one bowl instead of five cups, and costs less than a
      cake tin. Recipes are still written in cups, but cups are accurate only for liquids.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
